.\" Written from docs/index.html by go run ./tools/man; edit the manual, not this.
.TH CONN 1 "" "conn 0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- a station for directing many processes on one machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn down
.br
.B conn theme claude
.br
.B conn theme vim
.br
.B conn \-\-light
.br
.B conn \-\-dark
.br
.B conn \-\-help
.br
.B conn \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
The station shows what each process is doing and which one is waiting, and puts
the operator in front of it.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBPURPOSE.\fR This manual describes conn and its operation.
.TP
1-2.
\fBSCOPE.\fR The station serves one operator directing many processes on one
machine. It shows the processes running in each project, what each is doing, and
which one is waiting for the operator, and it puts the operator in front of any
of them. It does not edit files, run builds, or hold sessions. The processes do.
.TP
1-3.
\fBDEFINITIONS.\fR Terms used in this manual.
.RS
.TP
\fBOperator\fR
The person using conn.
.TP
\fBProcess\fR
A shell, a contact, or a command a shell is running. The unit the station lists
and reaches.
.TP
\fBContact\fR
A process connected to a non-human intelligence: Claude Code running in a
terminal. It works a project and can stop to wait for the operator.
.TP
\fBIntelligence\fR
The non-human intelligence at the other end of a contact. The station sees the
contact and not the intelligence.
.TP
\fBSession\fR
The conversation a contact carries, from raising it to ending it. Its record is
the transcript. A session outlives its contact and can be resumed.
.TP
\fBProject\fR
The directory a process runs in: a git repository, or a folder that holds
several repositories.
.TP
\fBStatus\fR
The state the station reports for a process: WORKING, ACTIVE, IDLE, or WAITING.
.TP
\fBWaiting\fR
The status of a contact that has stopped and needs an answer from the operator,
such as a permission or a question.
.TP
\fBConsole\fR
The page conn comes up on. It reads out the machine and gives a verdict.
.TP
\fBPanel\fR
The left pane of the window, forty-four columns wide. It shows one of the three
views and usually holds the keys.
.TP
\fBProcesses\fR
The view listing every process at each project, in the order they started.
.TP
\fBProjects\fR
The view listing every project under the roots, running or not.
.TP
\fBSessions\fR
The view listing the sessions at a project that can be resumed.
.TP
\fBBay\fR
The right pane of the window. It holds one process.
.TP
\fBReadout\fR
A page of labeled values on one process, shown in the bay.
.TP
\fBStatus line\fR
The line at the bottom of the window. It shows where the keys are, on the left.
.RE
.TP
1-4.
\fBINDICATIONS.\fR The station indicates two things: what each process is doing,
and which process is waiting. The processes view shows both: the status column
shows the first, and the word WAITING, blinking, shows the second. \fBtab\fR
goes to what is waiting from anywhere in the server (4-3).
.TP
1-5.
\fBCOMMANDS.\fR The command alone brings up the console and continues to
processes. Other commands are given by name after it. \fB\-\-light\fR and
\fB\-\-dark\fR set the ground, ahead of a command if one is given.
.PP
.I "SYNOPSIS"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn\fR
Brings up the console, then processes.
.TP
\fBconn down\fR
Takes the server down with everything in it, and reports what went.
.TP
\fBconn theme claude\fR
Writes conn's theme for Claude Code.
.TP
\fBconn theme vim\fR
Writes conn's colorscheme for nvim.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-light\fR
Sets the ground light.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-dark\fR
Sets the ground dark.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-help\fR
Prints this table.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-version\fR
Prints the build.
.RE
.SH THE STATION
.TP
2-1.
\fBARRANGEMENT.\fR The station is one window of a tmux server that conn holds.
The panel is on the left and the bay on the right. The status line runs under
both. Figure 2-1 shows the arrangement.
.PP
.RS
.nf
┌──────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
│ PANEL                        │ BAY                         │
│                              │                             │
│ KIND     COMMAND  AGE STATUS │ The process reached from    │
│                              │ the panel: a shell, a       │
│ w0zro/conn                   │ contact, a run, or the      │
│ SHELL    zsh      2H    IDLE │ readout. The keys are here  │
│▸ CONTACT claude   1H WAITING │ while a process is.         │
│                              │                             │
├──────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┤
│ STATUS LINE  PROCS                                         │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "FIGURE 2-1. STATION ARRANGEMENT"
.TP
2-2.
\fBTHE SERVER.\fR The station holds a tmux server of its own. Its socket is
under \fB~/.local/state/conn\fR, or where \fBCONN_SOCKET\fR says. The first
\fBconn\fR brings the server up and puts the terminal on it. A later \fBconn\fR
attaches to what is there. Everything in the server stays until the server is
taken down (5-2). A process that leaves the bay goes to a window of its own, out
of sight, and keeps running.
.TP
2-3.
\fBCOMING UP.\fR To bring up the station:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Type \fBconn\fR.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
The console comes up. It reads out the machine, runs the start-up checks, and
gives its verdict: ALL SYSTEMS NOMINAL, or the count of systems that are not.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press any key. The processes view comes up on the panel and the bay opens beside
it.
.RE
.TP
2-4.
\fBTHE CONSOLE.\fR \fBc\fR in the processes view brings the console back over
the whole window. Any key returns to processes.
.TP
2-5.
\fBTHE PREFIX.\fR Chords are given under \fBctrl\-space\fR. \fBCONN_PREFIX\fR
names another prefix, in the spelling tmux uses. While a chord is open, PREFIX
is lit on the status line. No other tmux key is bound.
.RS
.TP
\fBctrl\-space \-\fR
Puts the keys on the panel, from anywhere in the server.
.TP
\fBctrl\-space p\fR
Opens projects, from anywhere in the server (3-7).
.TP
\fBctrl\-space tab\fR
Goes to the contact that has waited longest (4-3).
.TP
\fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR
Goes to the process that was last in the bay (4-4).
.TP
\fBctrl\-space q\fR
Detaches (2-6).
.RE
.TP
2-6.
\fBSTANDING DOWN.\fR \fBq\fR on the panel detaches the terminal from the server.
\fBctrl\-space q\fR does the same from anywhere in it. The server and every
process in it keep running, and the next \fBconn\fR attaches to them. Ending the
server is procedure 5-2.
.TP
2-7.
\fBWITHOUT TMUX.\fR Without tmux on the path, the station shows the console and
the processes view and reaches nothing. On macOS the processes view needs
\fBlsof\fR to read the working directory of a process.
.TP
2-8.
\fBTHE GROUND.\fR The server comes up on a dark or a light ground. The first
time it rises it asks the terminal for its background and keeps the answer for
the life of the server. \fBconn \-\-light\fR and \fBconn \-\-dark\fR set the
ground instead, for a server coming up or one already up. Every pane takes the
new ground.
.TP
2-9.
\fBTHEMES.\fR A program that draws its own colors takes none of the server's.
\fBconn theme claude\fR writes a theme for Claude Code and offers to select it.
\fBconn theme vim\fR writes a colorscheme for nvim, taken with \fBcolorscheme
conn\fR. Each is drawn on the ground the server is on.
.TP
2-10.
\fBENVIRONMENT.\fR The station reads three variables.
.RS
.TP
\fBCONN_ROOTS\fR
The roots the projects view walks for repositories, as a path list.
\fB~/projects\fR when unset.
.TP
\fBCONN_SOCKET\fR
Where the server's socket is.
.TP
\fBCONN_PREFIX\fR
The prefix for chords, in the spelling tmux uses. \fBC\-Space\fR when unset.
.RE
.SH PROCESS PROCEDURES
.TP
3-1.
\fBPROCESSES.\fR The processes view lists every process at each project. A
project is titled by its path below the root; a project outside every root is
written from \fB~\fR. Under a project the processes are shown as a tree: a
shell, and what it runs indented under it. Each row shows the kind, the command,
the age, and the status. On the console, which is wider, the terminal is shown
too. A row keeps its position for as long as its process lives, oldest first,
and a new row goes on the end. With nothing running the view says NO PROCESSES.
.TP
3-2.
\fBSTATUS.\fR The last column of a row is its status.
.RS
.TP
\fBWORKING\fR
Did something between one reading and the next. A contact is WORKING when it is
mid-turn, or when a command is running under it.
.TP
\fBACTIVE\fR
Alive and doing nothing.
.TP
\fBIDLE\fR
A shell at its prompt, or a contact whose turn is over. Nothing is pending.
.TP
\fBWAITING\fR
A contact stopped on something it needs from the operator. The word blinks.
.TP
\fBSTOPPED\fR
Suspended. Shown as a chip.
.TP
\fBENDED\fR
Finished and not yet collected. Shown as a chip.
.RE
.TP
3-3.
\fBWHO IS WAITING.\fR Only a contact that reports its own status is ever
WAITING. Claude Code reports it, and reports when its status changed. A contact
the station cannot ask reads as WORKING or ACTIVE from the processor time it
spends.
.TP
3-4.
\fBKEYS.\fR The cursor is on one row. It follows its process from one reading to
the next.
.RS
.TP
\fBj, k\fR
Move the cursor. The arrows do the same.
.TP
\fBenter\fR
Puts the process under the cursor in the bay, with the keys in it.
.TP
\fBtab\fR
Goes to the contact that has waited longest (4-3).
.TP
\fBs\fR
Opens a shell at the project under the cursor.
.TP
\fBa\fR
Raises a contact at the project under the cursor (4-2).
.TP
\fBalt\-a\fR
Opens sessions for the project under the cursor (4-5).
.TP
\fBi\fR
Opens the readout on the row under the cursor. Pressed again, closes it (3-6).
.TP
\fBx\fR
Asks to end the process under the cursor (4-6).
.TP
\fBp\fR
Opens projects (3-7).
.TP
\fBc\fR
Brings the console back over the window (2-4).
.TP
\fBq\fR
Detaches (2-6).
.RE
.TP
3-5.
\fBHELD AND REPORTED.\fR A row the station holds a pane for is in the ink, and
\fBenter\fR reaches it. A row the station can only report, a terminal it did not
open, is dimmed. The cursor lands on it; no other key acts on it. The row in the
bay carries its kind in orange. Outside the server every row is reported.
.TP
3-6.
\fBTHE READOUT.\fR The readout is a page of labeled values on one process, shown
in the bay. It carries what the row has no room for: the whole command, the
directory, the time up and the processor time spent, what runs it and what it
runs; of a contact, the session it carries and what it is stopped on; of the
project, what git reports. To read a row:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Put the cursor on the row.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Press \fBi\fR. The readout opens in the bay. The keys stay on the panel.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
\fBj\fR and \fBk\fR move the readout with the cursor. \fBtab\fR carries it to
the contact that is waiting.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
Press \fBi\fR again. The readout closes. Where the row is one the station holds,
its process takes the bay.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
\fBconn readout 1234\fR, from a shell in the server, pins the readout to one
process instead of the cursor.
.TP
3-7.
\fBPROJECTS.\fR The projects view lists every project under the roots, whether
anything is running in it or not. A folder that holds several repositories is a
row with them under it. To open a process at a project:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Press \fBp\fR in the processes view, \fBalt\-p\fR from any view, or
\fBctrl\-space p\fR from anywhere in the server.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Type to narrow the rows. \fBbackspace\fR and \fBctrl\-u\fR edit the line. The
arrows, or \fBctrl\-p\fR and \fBctrl\-n\fR, move the cursor.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press \fBenter\fR. A shell opens at the project, and processes returns with it
in the bay. \fBctrl\-a\fR raises a contact there instead (4-2). \fBalt\-a\fR
opens sessions (4-5).
.TP
\fB4.\fR
\fBesc\fR returns to processes with nothing opened.
.RE
.TP
3-8.
\fBTHE STATUS LINE.\fR The status line is dark at rest. The left says where the
keys are, as a block of the orange with the word knocked out of it. Every
indication below wears the same block; the word says which. It is dark while the
keys are in the bay, and dark on the console. The right is empty.
.RS
.TP
\fBPREFIX\fR
A chord is open. The next key is one of 2-5.
.TP
\fBCOPY\fR
The pane with the keys is in copy mode.
.TP
\fBCONFIRM\fR
A kill is armed. The question stands beside it (4-6). It covers the word for the
view.
.TP
\fBPROCS\fR
The keys are on the panel, in the processes view.
.TP
\fBPROJECTS\fR
The keys are on the panel, in projects (3-7).
.TP
\fBSESSIONS\fR
The keys are on the panel, in sessions (4-5).
.RE
.SH CONTACT PROCEDURES
.TP
4-1.
\fBCONTACT.\fR A contact is a process connected to a non-human intelligence. The
station raises Claude Code, and the procedures of this section are written for
it. The station sees the contact and not the intelligence: what it lists,
reaches, and ends is the process. A contact the station did not raise is a
process like any other: it is reached, put in the bay, and ended the same way.
The station cannot ask it its status, so it is never WAITING.
.TP
4-2.
\fBRAISING A CONTACT.\fR To raise a contact at a project:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
In the processes view, put the cursor on any row of the project. Or open
projects (3-7) and put the cursor on the project.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Press \fBa\fR. In projects, press \fBctrl\-a\fR.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
A shell opens at the project running \fBclaude\fR, takes the bay, and has the
keys. Its row is in the processes view under the project, kind CONTACT.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
The station tells the contact where it is: that it runs in a pane of the
station, and how to start anything long-lived in a window of its own. A process
started that way has a row and can be reached.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
What the station tells the contact travels with conn. Nothing is written into
the settings of Claude Code.
.TP
4-3.
\fBANSWERING A CONTACT.\fR When a contact stops on something it needs from the
operator, its row reads WAITING and the word blinks. To answer it:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Press \fBtab\fR in the processes view, or \fBctrl\-space tab\fR from anywhere in
the server.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
The cursor goes to the contact that has waited longest. Its pane takes the bay
and the keys land in it.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Answer it.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
Press \fBtab\fR again for the next, longest first, and round again from the end.
.RE
.TP
4-4.
\fBWORKING TWO PROCESSES.\fR \fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR goes to the process
that was last in the bay, and pressed again comes back. A vacant bay and the
readout are not remembered. \fBalt\-o\fR does the same from the panel.
.TP
4-5.
\fBRESUMING A SESSION.\fR A session outlives its contact. Its transcript is
kept, and a new contact can resume it. To resume one:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
In the processes view, put the cursor on any row of the project. Or open
projects and put the cursor on the project.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Press \fBalt\-a\fR. The sessions view lists the sessions at the project, newest
first, each by its branch, the last thing it was asked, and its age. Type to
narrow the rows.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press \fBenter\fR. A shell opens running \fBclaude \-\-resume\fR on that session
and takes the bay.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
\fBesc\fR returns to processes with nothing resumed.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A session a live contact is carrying is not listed. The sessions view checks the
process table as well as the transcripts.
.TP
4-6.
\fBENDING A CONTACT.\fR To end a contact:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Put the cursor on its row and press \fBx\fR.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
CONFIRM lights on the status line, with the question beside it: END CLAUDE 1234
· X CONFIRMS · ANY OTHER KEY CANCELS.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press \fBx\fR, \fBy\fR, or \fBenter\fR. The contact is asked to end. The shell
it ran in is left at its prompt. Any other key cancels.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
The processes view reads again after a beat, and the row goes when the contact
has.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A contact ended this way keeps its session. Procedure 4-5 resumes it.
.SH EMERGENCY PROCEDURES
.TP
5-1.
\fBA PROCESS THAT WILL NOT END.\fR \fBx\fR sends the process under the cursor
SIGTERM. A bare shell is sent SIGKILL instead. A process that ignores SIGTERM
stays in the processes view. Reach it with \fBenter\fR and end it from inside.
.TP
5-2.
\fBTAKING THE STATION DOWN.\fR To end the server and everything in it:
.TP
\fBCAUTION\fR
\fBconn down\fR ends every process in the server: every shell, every contact,
and every run, in the bay or out of sight in a window of its own. Work that is
not saved is lost. A session can be resumed afterward (4-5). A run cannot.
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
From a terminal outside conn, or from a shell in it, type \fBconn down\fR.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
A line is printed for each window ended and one for the server.
.RE
.TP
5-3.
\fBTHE BAY EMPTIES.\fR When the process in the bay ends, the bay takes the next
process the station holds, from the cursor down and round again from the top.
With none to take, it shows VACANT. Any key in it puts the keys on the panel.
.TP
5-4.
\fBTHE KEYS ARE ELSEWHERE.\fR From any pane of the server, \fBctrl\-space \-\fR
puts the keys on the panel. From the panel, \fBenter\fR puts them in the bay.
.TP
5-5.
\fBTHE PROCESSES CANNOT BE READ.\fR When the process table cannot be read, the
processes view shows the reason as a chip in place of its rows and reads again
on the next beat. On macOS the usual reason is that \fBlsof\fR gave no answer in
time.
